WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:07.380
Hello again I'm Dustin Kirkland and I'm joined
this time by Ian Lewis, Developer Advocate

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.380
at Google.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.530
He is going to tell us a little about a new
open source container isolation sandbox called

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.599
gVisor.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:20.720
Right, so gVisor is a sandbox isolation project
for containers.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.750
It provides a lightweight execution mode for
containers, but also gives you very secure

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.430
isolation between those containers.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.640
It's really all about the security isn't it?

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.679
Exactly, so for things like containers they
provide isolation in the form of namespaces

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:43.719
and c groups and as a feature of the Linux
kernel but you are exposed to kernel bugs

00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:49.329
and things like that and unforeseen issues
as where they are to the Linux kernel, so

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:55.670
if any sort of new bug happens or that type
of thing a zero day happens you are exposed

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.719
to that and so what this does is actually
helps you mitigate those type of issues.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:03.530
Right, it reduces the surface, as I understand
it, right?

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.530
Right.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:10.680
So it actually provides a separate layer in
between the running application and the Linux

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.680
kernel,.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.079
And what is that layer?

00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:20.049
So that layer is actually a user space implementation
or user space kernel so to be a little bit

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:26.829
detailed it implements a lot of Linux or most
of the Linux syscalls and then provides user

00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:29.270
space virtualization for the container.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.270
Cool.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.799
And you've got a demo for us today?

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:35.070
Tell us a little about this demo and how it's
going to work.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.590
Yes, this particular demo is going to show
a kernel vulnerability and what we are going

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.710
to do is we are going to show how that vulnerability
can be used to break out of the container

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:49.140
and then out of a normal container that is
a Linux container that's using namespaces

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:56.049
and how that can actually be mitigated when
using something like - or when using gVisor.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:59.030
And this is a real vulnerability, not something
that you've instrumented?

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.170
Right.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.799
This is a vulnerability that has since been
patched obviously but it's one that if it

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:09.759
was released to it would have been Sure, it
would have been protected better by gVisor.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:11.269
By gVisor, exactly.

00:02:11.269 --> 00:02:12.269
Interesting.

00:02:12.269 --> 00:02:16.500
So what we are going to do is we are going
to take advantage of the dirty cow vulnerability

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.500
in the Linux kernel.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:24.370
What I have here is an OCI image or an OCI
bundle and in the configuration here we've

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:32.819
got the UID and GID set to a user and here
we're going to run a dirty cow vulnerability

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:37.769
and this is going to write to a file that
is controlled by root from a user from within

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:39.030
a container.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:46.390
So when they run this using runc, this takes
a little while but what's happening here is

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:52.122
that we are doing a couple syscalls in parallel
in order to trigger a race condition in the

00:02:52.122 --> 00:02:57.549
Linux Kernel and that's going to allow us
to write to a file that's controlled by root

00:02:57.549 --> 00:03:01.840
and effectively get a privilege escalation.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:10.569
Okay, so the exploit has run and so what we're
going to do here is we're going to check the

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:12.409
file that we're going to write to.

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:19.140
So here we were trying to write to the password
file and in this case our particular exploit

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.740
wrote to the beginning of the password file.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:28.180
So here you can imagine that you can write
an entirely new line for our road user and

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.239
give a new password and essentially get privilege
escalation.

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:40.000
So, what we're going to do here is we're going
to run the exact same vulnerability in the

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.640
gVisor sandbox so here I have the exact same
OCI bundle, so we'll look at the config.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:53.690
Here we have the exact same user ID setup,
UID and GID, as well as the same exploit that

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.530
we're going to try to run here.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:03.630
And so what we'll do is we'll use the runsc
command to invoke gVisor and we'll run this

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:08.280
with the ptrace and strace commands and what
this will do is allow us to see the syscalls

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.109
that are being made by the exploit.

00:04:11.109 --> 00:04:13.390
Here we can see a lot of debug.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:19.200
This particular exploit is running, is calling
the syscalls in a loop.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.480
In a tight loop in two different threads and
so we can see those syscalls being made here

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.980
being intercepted by gVisor.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:36.480
Because we have this extra layer between the
application and the Linux kernel here the

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.480
gVisor provides, we can intercept those and
prevent the vulnerability from actually happening.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:48.020
So now we've dropped out of the command and
we're just going to take a look at our password

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:52.500
file to see if it has actually been written
to.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:59.830
So here we're going to look at the contents
of the file and we can see here that the exploit

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.300
was not actually able to write to the file.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:09.360
So that's super interesting actually, if a
user interested in downloading gVisor, maybe

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.980
running it today, what's the minimum requirements.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:13.900
How does someone get started?

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:20.120
Right now it's a normal open source project
so we have that released on GitHub so you

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:28.120
can check that out and compile and run gVisor
on your own it's very similar in the end to

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:34.220
runc in the sense that it provides two things
- it provides a kind of library that you can

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:41.150
use to run sandbox containers as well a command
called runsc which is very similar to runc

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.950
in the sense that it's a drop in, kind of
replacement for runc so you can use things

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.130
like Docker to actually run a container.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.520
Okay, great.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.510
And if someone wants to contribute to this
project?

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:55.770
It's an open source project as much as Kubernetes
is an open source project?

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:03.430
Sure, absolutely, so you can contribute to
the project by engaging with us through GitHub

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:13.010
and submitting issues and patches there and
joining in the community by providing feedback

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:14.010
through GitHub.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.360
That's great, thanks Ian.

